Final crate graph and deletion boundary

The workspace contains 21 packages: the fixed-tenant `mfm-app`, two binaries, EVM/Portfolio
domains, EVM live adapter, keystore/signing primitives, canonical/ids/values/facts/journal/
program/replay/runtime/store kernel crates, and the two PostgreSQL storage crates. The cutover
deletes the former Bitcoin packages, the authority-seal/certification/specification packages,
`mfm-integration-tests`, and `mfm-wallet-authority-provider-test-support` package surfaces. It
also deletes the authority/runtime/store guard family (`authority-integration`, the runtime-
authority and store authority feature guards) and the old structured five-family operation, certification,
specification, provider-authority, policy, and generic append surfaces. Remaining test-support
features are narrow test-only wiring, not production authority bridges.

Public API before and after

The pre-cutover surface exposed structured operation/runtime/history/specification/certification
types, generic semantic append paths, policy/credential/principal transport inputs, provider
authority and currentness lineage, multiple observation families, and Bitcoin workflows. The
current surface exposes one `Application::for_tenant` facade, singular `AdmitRunRequest`, the
`Runtime::{admission,resume_run}` lifecycle and exhaustive `RuntimeStep`/`ResumeStep` outcomes,
`StructuredStore::open` over one mechanical backend, split non-Clone history/configuration/audit
ports, `ProgramDocument`/`Program`/`ProgramCatalog`/`ProgramIngress`, `RunFrame` with exactly
`RunAdmitted`, `StatePrepared`, and `StateConcluded`, callback-free `PortableRun`, and the
redaction-safe `PostgresStore` adapter.

The following values are intentionally opaque or affine: `Program`, `ProgramRef`, the
catalog-branded `QualifiedTypedValue<T>` and its private erased `QualifiedValue`, `QualifiedRun`,
`OpenedStructuredStore` and its split ports, `RunSession`, `PreparedExecution`, `CommittedCall`,
pending-conclusion owners, configuration append owners, and prior-fact continuations. Their
constructors, brands, and consuming transitions prevent foreign-catalog/store transposition,
duplicate conclusion ownership, and retry authority minting. No public unchecked downcast or free
erasure constructor exists.

Persisted and protocol identities

- Run records are the three append-only families `RunAdmitted`, `StatePrepared`, and
  `StateConcluded::{Pure,Access}`; each frame is canonical, content-addressed, and exact-head
  chained. A run is tenant/scope/epoch bound.
- PostgreSQL activation is the fresh three-family schema contract
  `mfm.structured-run-history-postgres.v8`, admitted only under `PrimaryCrashRestart`, after
  migration, schema/identity checks, `fsync=on`, `full_page_writes=on`, non-off
  `synchronous_commit`, and a fresh logical scope/writer epoch.
- Portable export is `mfm.portable-run.v5`, content-addressed under the `mfm.portable-run` v1
  schema, and accepts only the three-family canonical stream with object/fact closure.
- Binding descriptors use `mfm.execution-binding` v1; configuration revisions use
  `mfm.configuration` v1; fact streams use `mfm.fact-stream` v1.
- Supported entry identities are `mfm.evm/submit-transaction@1` with operation
  `mfm.evm.submit-transaction@1` and `mfm.portfolio/snapshot@1` with operation
  `mfm.portfolio.snapshot@1`. The EVM broadcast capability is
  `mfm.evm.capability.broadcast-transaction@1` and is `EntryOnce`.
- Activation is fixed-tenant App construction plus one catalog/store identity; live embedding
  registers the exact State, adapter, and binding assembly; admission persists one domain-owned
  C0; direct-new preparation persists the deterministic call identity before provider entry;
  resume accepts only the same catalog/store scope, epoch, and tenant. Binding replacement
  rotates scope/epoch and makes old runs replay/read-only.

Capacity and race evidence

The final `capacity-envelope` task passed all three leaves. Its recorded values are:

- EVM: 4 declarations, 6,840 Program bytes, C0 262,296 bytes, Cn 262,382 bytes;
- Portfolio: 64 collections, 64 total sources, 963 declarations, 1,566,954 Program bytes, C0
  6,284 bytes, Cn 12,736 bytes;
- Runtime: retained hot head 2 / 4,063 frame bytes / 11 context bytes; one-shot cold resume head
  3 / 6,194 frame bytes / 11 context bytes. Linux `VmHWM` samples in the focused run were
  7,507,968 bytes after hot advancement and 7,544,832 bytes after cold resume. These are
  characterization values, not latency thresholds; canonical retained bytes are the portable
  bound and `VmHWM` includes the test harness;
- configuration: exactly 1,024 revisions, exactly 16 MiB one-revision bytes, exactly 64 MiB stream
  bytes, with independent bound-plus-one rejection;
- outer and domain bounds: 32 MiB frame, 65,536 frames, 1,048,576 objects, 512 MiB run bytes,
  64 EVM sources, 3 Read/absorbing attempts, and 1 EntryOnce attempt, each with exact/+1 owner
  tests; and
- conclusion reservation: selected preparation owns the complete declared maximum, replacement
  transfers it, conclusion discharges it, and a superseded conclusion does not release or mint a
  second liability.

The PostgreSQL managed lane now uses an explicit filesystem rendezvous so two independent child
processes observe the same seeded head before each admission, preparation, replacement, and
conclusion append. Each stage records exactly one `NewlyCommitted` and one `StaleHead` result and
then qualifies the winning prefix. The isolated Linux primary probe commits admission,
preparation, and conclusion, kills the primary, restarts it, and qualifies the recovered prefix.
Runtime's provider-entry/preparation/interpretation counters remain separately exact-one for the
direct-new winner; a stale process never receives a call owner.

Product reduction and concept ownership

Counting Rust files below `bin/` and `crates/` (including inline tests), the pre-cutover parent
contained 158,560 lines and 1,470 public declaration lines; the final tree contains 32,644 lines
and 401 public declaration lines (the declaration count is a stable grep-based comparison, not a
semantic API inventory). The product-only cutover delta is 25,169 insertions and 154,349
deletions across 466 paths. The old workspace had 28 members; the final graph has 21.

Net-new concepts have one owner:

- Runtime lifecycle/coordinator and affine session own admission, resume, drive, suspension, and
  owner fate because State semantics must not schedule or perform ambient IO;
- composite StructuredStore and mechanical backend SPI own identity, CAS, append atomicity,
  qualification, configuration, facts, and audit because adapters must not duplicate semantic
  storage rules;
- `PreparationRef`, selected-preparation liability, and conclusion classifications belong to
  Store because only Store can observe the exact durable prefix and reserve/discharge capacity;
- binding/evidence descriptors belong to Journal/domain capability contracts because they are the
  immutable, secret-free propositions needed for callback-free recovery;
- domain cumulative contexts and typed caller continuations belong to EVM/Portfolio because the
  kernel cannot interpret domain fields; and
- `ProcessRaceStage` and `capacity-envelope` are test/artifact concepts only, because production
  CAS and capacity ownership already exist and must not gain a coordinator or a new public knob.

